            "TimesLTStd-Roman"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            5.02,
            7.0,
            7.03,
            10.0
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "a sum of products of discrete Gaussians",
          "bbox": [
            96.38,
            338.12,
            254.12,
            348.12
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "TimesLTStd-Roman"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            10.0
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "j=1",
          "bbox": [
            256.43,
            348.39,
            268.95,
            356.09
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMR7",
            "TimesLTStd-Italic",
            "TimesLTStd-Roman"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            7.0,
            7.03
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "of the discrete Gaussian is the key ingredient in the proof of theorem 7.",
          "bbox": [
            96.38,
            357.32,
            381.07,
            367.32
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "TimesLTStd-Roman"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            10.0
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Theorem 7 is of interest in combinatorics, statistical physics and quantum field theory",
          "bbox": [
            108.33,
            369.32,
            468.42,
            379.32
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "TimesLTStd-Roman"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            10.0
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "(QFT). Several recent papers in mathematical physics, notably [HK20, IK22, Gr23], have",
          "bbox": [
            96.38,
            381.32,
            468.43,
            391.32
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "TimesLTStd-Roman"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            10.0
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "extended theorem 7. A problem is raised in [RV15] in connection with their approach to a",
          "bbox": [
            96.38,
            393.32,
            468.42,
            403.32
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "TimesLTStd-Roman"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            10.0
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "combinatorial QFT which theorem 7 addresses just a part, aside from the 2D case that was",
          "bbox": [
            96.38,
            405.32,
            468.43,
            415.32
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "TimesLTStd-Roman"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            10.0
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "already known as just mentioned.",
          "bbox": [
            96.38,
            417.32,
            230.28,
            427.32
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "TimesLTStd-Roman"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            10.0
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "We point out that in higher dimensions d > 2 the lead terms in the asymptotics of theorem 7",
          "bbox": [
            108.33,
            428.33,
            468.42,
            439.32
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMMI10",
            "TimesLTStd-Italic",
            "TimesLTStd-Roman"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            10.0
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "were established in [SW00, SS01, Ly05]; see also their bibliographies for further references.",
          "bbox": [
            96.38,
            441.32,
            468.42,
            451.32
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "TimesLTStd-Roman"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            10.0
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "In particular, the formula of Sokal-Starinets involves the I-Bessel function in the same way",
          "bbox": [
            96.38,
            453.32,
            468.43,
            463.32
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "TimesLTStd-Italic",
            "TimesLTStd-Roman"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            10.0
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "as in [CJK10], but Sokal-Starinets have another way of interpreting the appearance of the I-",
          "bbox": [
            96.38,
            465.32,
            468.42,
            475.32
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "TimesLTStd-Italic",
            "TimesLTStd-Roman"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            10.0
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Bessel, presumably not as a discrete Gaussian. Detailed asymptotics for more general lattice",
          "bbox": [
            96.38,
            477.32,
            468.42,
            487.32
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "TimesLTStd-Roman"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            10.0
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "domains in 2D are obtained in the work of Kenyon, see [Ke00].",
          "bbox": [
            96.38,
            489.32,
            350.29,
            499.32
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
